# PART D — AUTHENTICATION ARCHITECTURE

# 5. Authentication architecture

Use the following conceptual architecture:

```text
                    React Frontend
                          │
                          │ HTTPS
                          ▼
                       NGINX
                          │
                          ▼
                       FastAPI
                          │
                ┌─────────┴─────────┐
                │                   │
                ▼                   ▼
        Authentication         Authorization
           Phase 2              Phase 3
                │
                ▼
             Service
                │
                ▼
           Repository
                │
                ▼
           PostgreSQL
```

Authentication should be separated into:

```text
Authentication
        ↓
Who are you?

Authorization
        ↓
What are you allowed to do?
```

Phase 2 implements authentication.

Phase 3 will implement authorization/RBAC.

---

# 6. Authentication technology

Use:

```text
FastAPI
Pydantic
SQLAlchemy
PostgreSQL
JWT
Secure password hashing
Redis
```

Recommended JWT implementation:

```text
python-jose
```

or another well-maintained JWT library compatible with the project.

Password hashing should use a modern password hashing algorithm such as:

```text
Argon2
```

or an appropriately configured:

```text
bcrypt
```

Prefer Argon2 when practical.

Never store passwords as plaintext.

---

# PART E — USER MODEL

# 7. Create authentication user model

Phase 2 may introduce the foundational `users` table.

Create a proper SQLAlchemy model.

Conceptually:

```text
users
```

Possible fields:

```text
id
uuid
email
password_hash
first_name
last_name
is_active
is_verified
is_locked
failed_login_attempts
last_login_at
password_changed_at
created_at
updated_at
```

Do not add unnecessary employee-specific fields.

Employee-specific information belongs to Phase 4.

---

# 8. User ID

Use a scalable identifier strategy.

Prefer:

```text
UUID
```

or another globally unique identifier.

The architecture should work correctly when the platform contains:

```text
1 user
10,000 users
1,000,000+ users
```

Do not expose database implementation details unnecessarily.

---

# 9. Email uniqueness

User email should have an appropriate unique constraint.

Authentication should treat email consistently.

For example:

```text
Neeraj@example.com
neeraj@example.com
```

should not accidentally become two independent login identities if the application uses case-insensitive email identity.

Implement appropriate normalization.

---

# 10. Password storage

Never store:

```text
password
```

directly.

Store:

```text
password_hash
```

Example conceptual flow:

```text
User password
      ↓
Password hasher
      ↓
Argon2/bcrypt
      ↓
password_hash
      ↓
PostgreSQL
```

At login:

```text
Entered password
      ↓
Password verifier
      ↓
Stored password hash
      ↓
Match / Reject
```

---

# PART F — JWT ARCHITECTURE

# 11. Token architecture

Use two-token authentication:

```text
Access Token
Refresh Token
```

Conceptual flow:

```text
Login
  │
  ├── Access Token
  │
  └── Refresh Token
```

Access tokens should be short-lived.

Example configuration:

```text
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
```

Refresh tokens should have a longer lifetime.

Example:

```text
REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=7
```

These must be configurable through environment variables.

Do not hard-code expiration periods.

---

# 12. Access token

The access token should contain only necessary claims.

Potential claims:

```text
sub
iat
exp
type
jti
```

Example conceptual payload:

```json
{
  "sub": "user-uuid",
  "type": "access",
  "jti": "token-id",
  "iat": 1234567890,
  "exp": 1234569690
}
```

Do not put sensitive information into JWT payloads.

Do not store:

```text
password
password hash
security answers
private personal data
```

inside JWT claims.

---

# 13. Refresh token

Refresh tokens must be handled more carefully than access tokens.

Prefer a secure design where refresh tokens can be revoked.

A recommended architecture is:

```text
Refresh Token
      ↓
hashed token / token identifier
      ↓
Redis or PostgreSQL
      ↓
Session / token record
```

Do not make refresh-token revocation impossible.

---

# 14. Token rotation

Implement refresh-token rotation.

Conceptually:

```text
Refresh Token A
       ↓
Refresh API
       ↓
Invalidate A
       ↓
Generate B
       ↓
Return new access + refresh token
```

If an old refresh token is reused after rotation, the system should be able to detect it and invalidate the corresponding session/token family.

This is important for production security.

---

# 15. JWT secret

Never hard-code:

```text
JWT_SECRET
```

Use environment configuration.

Example:

```text
JWT_SECRET=
JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30
REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_DAYS=7
```

If asymmetric signing is selected later, the configuration should allow migration to:

```text
RS256
```

or another appropriate algorithm.

Do not expose secrets in logs.

---

# PART G — AUTHENTICATION API

# 16. API versioning

Continue using:

```text
/api/v1/
```

Authentication endpoints should be grouped under:

```text
/api/v1/auth/
```

---

